# Mesh-aware quick-swap icon fitting implementation plan

Source proposal: [MeshAwareIconFitting.proposal.md](MeshAwareIconFitting.proposal.md).
Status: planned; every implementation and acceptance task remains outstanding.

## Goal and governing constraints

Fit complete supported quick-swap item models inside their visible wedges using mesh vertices and the actual engine GUI draw state. Preserve normal orientation when it fits; rotate long models at nominal size before considering shrinking. Never enlarge beyond nominal size or label an unverified fallback as fitted.

The proposal is authoritative. Candidate selection, equipment operations, ordering, input, and hit testing retain their existing owners and behavior. Tool-mode artwork and center labels do not automatically opt in. Pixel scanning, alpha-mask readback, framebuffer analysis for bounds, GPU-buffer readback, global upload capture, and arbitrary-mod compatibility are outside scope. Existing capture, halo, and stencil rendering remain in use.

Implementation follows repository instructions: documented classes/methods, visibility-first regions, comments explaining nontrivial logic, focused files, directional dependencies, and thin composition roots. Build and test tools run through subagents. Planning terminology belongs only in planning documents.

Bounds calculations must use SIMD-accelerated `System.Numerics` vector types and their component-wise `Min`/`Max` operations rather than custom scalar min/max accumulation. This does not replace projected-hull fitting with an axis-aligned box or establish an unmeasured performance claim.

## Open decisions and evidence boundaries

- Installed-engine findings in the proposal came from version 1.22.7 metadata/IL inspection; verify the current assembly before relying on them.
- Resolve the earliest installation point, completed GUI matrix hook, update offset/count semantics, disposal observation, and thread ownership before implementing their consumers.
- Choose bounded translation/angle search, scale refinement assumptions, budgets, and deterministic tolerances; containment is guaranteed only for accepted results, while packing quality remains approximate.
- Establish detection and registration contracts for custom renderers and animated draws before granting support.
- Decide whether a single annular wedge receives a tested safe region or explicitly falls back. Wider sectors and center discs require explicit handling.
- Headless validation cannot establish in-game appearance or GPU cost. Visual acceptance is user-run; this plan does not authorize launching the game.

## Phase 1: Verify engine boundaries and settle integration contracts

Outcome: downstream work has verified hook, lifetime, and support contracts rather than assumed engine behavior.

- [ ] Inspect the current installed engine and relevant source; record findings in this plan or the source proposal, without creating an unrelated audit artifact.
  - [ ] Verify `UploadMultiTextureMesh` texture splitting, platform upload calls, and item/block/alternate-variant inventory creation paths.
  - [ ] Identify installation early enough to observe inventory creation even when quick-swap starts disabled.
  - [ ] Verify tracked platform position updates, destination-offset units, counts, allocations, index draw ranges, disposal coverage, and thread ownership.
  - [ ] Identify the completed GUI matrix boundary after replacement mesh/transform callbacks; verify separate position and lighting submissions and supported projection behavior.
  - [ ] Establish how custom renderers, overlays, matrix changes, extra meshes, and joint animation are detected; define conservative unsupported behavior.
- [ ] Define focused owner boundaries from the proposal: geometry cache, mesh adapter, GUI fitting adapter, projected geometry helper, radial fitter, quick-tool opt-in, and menu context.
  - [ ] Place engine cache/adapters in an item-rendering domain and generic math in the radial-menu domain; retain `IRadialMenuIcon` with optional capability/context.
  - [ ] Define fit status, angle, positive scale, pivot, and translation contracts; define explicit geometry/renderer registration and invalidation.
  - [ ] Decide the initial supported wedge constructions and bounded solver approach, including feasibility assumptions.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Hook/update/lifecycle decisions have current source or installed-assembly evidence; unknown cases explicitly fall back.
- [ ] Contracts preserve a single ordinary draw and its callbacks, lighting, render-state restoration, and engine mesh ownership.
- [ ] Review verifies these decisions against the complete proposal before foundational implementation proceeds.

## Phase 2: Capture and maintain owned mesh positions

Depends on Phase 1. Outcome: supported inventory meshes expose complete, trustworthy CPU geometry snapshots.

- [ ] Install a narrow capture scope around `UploadMultiTextureMesh` and observe successful child platform uploads.
  - [ ] Copy only valid positions per child; retain neither caller arrays, whole `MeshData`, textures, unused capacity, nor duplicate aggregate snapshots.
  - [ ] Key snapshots by `MeshRef` object identity with revisions; aggregate identity includes every child and revision.
  - [ ] Support explicit registration for manually assembled references; missing children make the whole model unsupported.
- [ ] Maintain tracked geometry through updates and resource lifetime.
  - [ ] Merge verified partial position ranges and publish complete snapshots atomically under established thread ownership.
  - [ ] Preserve positions for color/UV/normal-only changes; retain across index-only changes only when the verified draw-range contract permits it.
  - [ ] Invalidate on unknown allocations/contents, unsupported position updates, invalid ranges, and non-finite coordinates.
  - [ ] Use weak mesh ownership and observable disposal cleanup; never dispose engine-owned meshes or key by reused GL handles.
  - [ ] Separate menu/projected-fit resets from snapshots for still-live meshes during transitions/reloads; missing observations remain unsupported until registration or upload.
- [ ] Through a subagent, validate copied-array ownership, valid counts/capacity, child updates, partial and non-position updates, invalidation, mesh replacement, disposal, handle reuse, reloads, and enabling after initial loading.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Supported meshes have complete revisioned positions without retaining unrelated terrain/GUI uploads.
- [ ] Lifecycle tests demonstrate no stale or concurrently mutated snapshots and safe unsupported status when completeness is unknown.

## Phase 3: Project geometry and prove safe-region containment

Depends on Phases 1–2. Outcome: pure geometry helpers can measure the full model and conservatively judge containment.

- [ ] Project all selected child positions with the actual model-view/projection matrices and viewport mapping.
  - [ ] Account for item/block origins, GUI translation, base rotation/nonuniform scale, screen Y, and homogeneous division.
  - [ ] Reject invalid/unsupported projection, including crossing the homogeneous clipping boundary.
  - [ ] Deduplicate points and build a 2D convex hull with explicit point/line handling; do not substitute a projected 3D AABB or inspect transparency.
  - [ ] Use appropriate `System.Numerics` vector types and their SIMD-accelerated component-wise `Min`/`Max` operations for bounds accumulation; verify valid-point handling and bounds correctness in the focused geometry checks.
  - [ ] Retain source positions for orientation changes and record a geometry-derived pivot.
- [ ] Derive a convex safe region from the shader contour and shared `RadialMenuWedgeStyle` values.
  - [ ] For ordinary sectors at most 180 degrees, intersect an inset outer disk, separator-adjusted angular half-planes, and the bisector tangent half-plane excluding the inner disk.
  - [ ] Reserve halo, border, padding, full corner radius, and numeric/rendering clearance; detect empty usable area.
  - [ ] Explicitly handle single annular wedges, wider sectors, and center discs according to Phase 1 decisions; preserve center artwork and use fallback for unsupported annular cases.
  - [ ] Validate every accepted transformed hull vertex with numerical slack in the convex region.
- [ ] Through a subagent, validate asymmetric/diagonal/multi-part models, transparent planes, degeneracies, projection transforms, and invalid homogeneous coordinates.
- [ ] Through a subagent, independently sample shader-contour edges/interiors across supported sizes, including inner-circle edge crossings, angular boundaries, separators, outer arcs, rounded corners, and halo/border clearance.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Projection and containment receipts cover the actual supported transform/size range; independent contour checks corroborate the solver predicate.
- [ ] Unsupported projections and wedge constructions cannot yield fitted status.

## Phase 4: Implement deterministic rotation-first fitting

Depends on Phase 3. Outcome: the fitter returns validated transforms with full-size rotation preferred over shrinking.

- [ ] Implement a bounded placement/rotation/scale search independent of engine and inventory policy.
  - [ ] Start at nominal size, zero rotation, and preferred midpoint; try recentering and feasible translations before rotation.
  - [ ] At nominal size include radial/tangential long-axis alignments, an angular sweep, and local refinement with translation feasibility at each angle.
  - [ ] Only shrink after no tested full-size orientation fits; do not shrink long models merely because their normal orientation fails.
  - [ ] Use scale refinement only with established feasibility assumptions; heuristic failure is not proof that no placement exists.
  - [ ] Rank full-size results first, then larger scale, smaller absolute rotation, and smaller midpoint displacement, with deterministic ties and tolerances; cap scale at one.
  - [ ] Return fitted/unsupported/invalid-geometry/insufficient-space statuses with angle, positive scale, pivot, and complete translation; validate before returning fitted status.
  - [ ] Bound work and describe quality as approximate rather than globally optimal.
- [ ] Through a subagent, validate normal-orientation fits, placement-only fits, long models rotating at scale one instead of shrinking, necessary shrinking, deterministic ties, bounded work, and empty regions.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] A feasible tested full-size rotated result always outranks a smaller less-rotated result.
- [ ] Every accepted result independently passes containment, and failed search is described within its actual guarantees.

## Phase 5: Integrate fitting into the ordinary GUI draw

Depends on Phases 2–4. Outcome: quick-swap icons use fitted geometry before existing capture/halo processing with safe fallback.

- [ ] Add quick-tool scoped opt-in carrying desired size and the menu's actual wedge geometry through an optional capability/context.
  - [ ] Consume resolved `ModelRef` and completed matrices from that same draw; never repeat `GetItemStackRenderInfo`, `OnBeforeRender`, or rendering to measure.
  - [ ] Apply `placement + scale * rotation(angle) * (projectedPoint - pivot)` through a screen-space position transform derived from active projection, rather than assumed model-space Z rotation.
  - [ ] Include recentering and placement in translation; omit the category head offset only for successful fitting.
  - [ ] Preserve lighting matrices without mutating shared arrays, depth ordering, and engine state restoration.
  - [ ] Make scopes nested-call safe and exception-safe; missing/incompatible hooks emit bounded diagnostics and retain ordinary rendering.
- [ ] Preserve the existing capture, halo, and stencil pipeline without a second image transform.
- [ ] Enforce support boundaries before exposing fitted status.
  - [ ] Cover observed default item/block/alternate meshes and tracked callback replacements with their actual transforms.
  - [ ] Verify registered renderers that consume supplied meshes/matrices, including the liquid renderer with stack-size display disabled, before granting support.
  - [ ] Require full geometry contracts for extra meshes, overlays, or changed matrices; require evaluated geometry or conservative envelopes for deformation, otherwise fallback.
  - [ ] Untracked/partly tracked references and unknown mutations use explicit registration/invalidation or fallback; never infer animation safety from non-null `ModelRef`.
  - [ ] Fallback retains existing offsets/rendering/stencil and may clip; empty regions report insufficient space, not fitted.
- [ ] Through a subagent, validate final replacement mesh/transform use, single callback invocation, nested/failing scope cleanup, ordinary inventory rendering, and each supported/unsupported rendering case.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Supported quick-swap draws are fitted before halo generation without altering ordinary inventory draws or menu behavior.
- [ ] Unsupported paths remain usable and cannot falsely report complete containment.

## Phase 6: Stabilize hover, resizing, and caches

Depends on Phase 5. Outcome: fitting stays contained and stable through layout changes without repeated unchanged-frame geometry work.

- [ ] Supply actual ring radii, start angle, direction, separator angle, screen radius, and hover geometry; retain nominal sizing from `RadialMenuLayout`.
- [ ] Establish containment throughout hover, accounting for pixel-based border/corner/halo widths.
  - [ ] Reuse the resting fit with the existing hover transform only when valid for the complete range.
  - [ ] Otherwise update placement/scale as needed while retaining orientation where feasible; verify intermediate states.
- [ ] Cache projected hulls and fit results by relevant geometry revisions, effective GUI orientation, projection/GUI scale, menu size, wedge count/placement, and style clearance.
  - [ ] Avoid retessellation, vertex scans, hull construction, and angle searches on unchanged frames.
  - [ ] Menu-center translation alone must not rebuild a local hull; invalidate fit caches on resource replacement/shutdown without erasing still-live snapshots.
- [ ] Through a subagent, validate supported wedge counts, menu-size limits, GUI scales, viewport changes, resizing, screen-edge placement, hover transitions, and cache invalidation/reuse.

Completion criteria:

- [ ] Complete hover/layout coverage demonstrates containment and stable orientation within supported cases.
- [ ] Instrumented unchanged-frame checks demonstrate reuse without hidden geometry/search work.

## Phase 7: Review, measure, and obtain user-run acceptance

Depends on Phases 1–6. Outcome: completion claims have focused implementation evidence, independent review, and separately identified live acceptance.

- [ ] Run appropriate build and focused automated checks through subagents; reconcile failures and rerun only affected checks after fixes.
- [ ] Obtain an independent implementation review against the complete proposal and this plan; resolve discrepancies before marking implementation complete.
- [ ] Measure first-open work separately from unchanged-frame work, retained vertices/bytes and overhead, cache misses, update/reload costs, and CPU/GPU costs where actual measurements are available.
  - [ ] Distinguish measurements from estimates, including the `12 * vertexCount` raw-position storage estimate; report unsupported coverage and search-quality limitations.
- [ ] Obtain user-run visual acceptance for long tools, broad shields, bows, asymmetric models, block-like lights, and stack-dependent replacements.
  - [ ] Verify full visibility, rotation before shrinking, constant halo thickness, unchanged shading/depth, stable orientation, screen edges, resizing, and intermediate hover states.
  - [ ] Record live evidence separately from source/unit/integration receipts; leave live acceptance outstanding if no user-run result is supplied.
- [ ] Update proposal status/support boundaries and link the implementation plan from the existing project entry without claiming broader radial-menu artwork support.

## Final acceptance

- [ ] Every proposal requirement maps to implemented behavior or an explicit proposal-permitted unsupported path/deferral with rationale; core requirements are not silently deferred.
- [ ] Focused build/test receipts and independent review are complete, with no unresolved containment or ordinary-rendering regression.
- [ ] User-run visual acceptance and required measurements are recorded with their evidence boundaries; headless results are not substituted for visual/GPU acceptance.
- [ ] Long models rotate at nominal size whenever the tested search finds a feasible full-size fit; shrinking remains the last resort.
- [ ] Planning documents accurately distinguish implemented, unsupported, measured, and still-outstanding work.
